      sources: "Sources: Chapter 08, “Checklist”; “Continue the book.”",
      say: `Here's what done looks like. The licensing decision is recorded. Every in-scope app is top-tier, single-tenant, listed by its enterprise application ID, in exactly one ring, and everything out of scope names the control that covers it instead. The egress location holds only the workload ranges and isn't trusted. Every policy started report-only. Each ring was enforced in its own change, on two clean weeks of reviewed evidence, with the alert already in place. Next, Chapter 9 takes on AI agent identities. Thanks for watching.`,
      detail: `Checklist: D-N4 is recorded in the scope file, and the tenant holds Workload Identities Premium. Every in-scope service principal is Tier 1, single-tenant, an application registered here, listed by its enterprise application object ID, in exactly one ring. Every managed identity, multitenant, SaaS, agent and legacy service principal in the tenant has an out-of-scope record naming the control that covers it and who reviewed it. NL-WorkloadEgress holds only the workloads' egress ranges and is untrusted. The pre-merge check runs on every pull request that touches workload-ca/ or the scope file, and has no Finding. Every new policy appeared in the tenant as report-only, and every plan was read as a dry run first. Each ring's sign-in summary covers at least 14 days, shows every principal Clear, and its SHA-256 was recorded by a reviewer. Each enforcement was its own change, under D-N5, with -AllowEnforce, one ring at a time; every exclusion has an owner, a reason and an end date. The block alert is deployed to security operations before the first ring is enforced. The read app holds only the read permissions; the write permission exists only on the identity that applies. The pinned Learn page matches its manifest, and every upstream commit since the pin has been reviewed. Next: Chapter 09 — AI Agent Identities.`,
